*.sh   text eol=lf
scripts/dev-hooks/* text eol=lf
# -text, NOT `text eol=crlf`. `eol` is a CHECKOUT filter: it makes a clone
# right and leaves every export wrong, because `git archive` -- which is what
# GitHub's "Source code (zip)" on a release is -- writes the blob verbatim.
# Users install from that zip, and cmd.exe seeks `goto` labels by byte offset:
# on an LF-only batch file it lands mid-line, so a `set` is skipped and the
# next line runs with an empty variable. Measured on a Surface Book 3,
# 2026-08-18: the SAME install_windows.bat failed at the venv step as LF and
# completed as CRLF, line endings the only difference. Storing CRLF in the
# blob is what makes clone, archive and zip all agree.
# test_windows_scripts_are_crlf.py pins this.
*.bat  -text
*.ps1  -text
# Consumed inside the Linux images built by docker-compose.yml, so they
# must stay LF even when checked out with core.autocrlf=true.
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.conf text eol=lf
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.ico  binary
